    rule lives.
  - [38;5;153mnotifications.js[39m — fan-out. Takes an event plus the resolved subscriber set, returns notification records. Owns the
    two rules that are easy to get wrong: [1mnever notify the actor of their own change[0m, and one notification per (user,
    event) — no duplicates when a user is both creator and assignee.

  [1mPorts[0m ([38;5;153msrc/ports/[39m) — the interface the domain depends on. One file, [38;5;153mrepository.js[39m, documenting the async contract:
  [38;5;153mloadTasks[39m, [38;5;153msaveTask[39m, [38;5;153mloadSubscriptions[39m, [38;5;153msaveSubscription[39m, [38;5;153mloadNotifications[39m, [38;5;153mappendNotifications[39m, [38;5;153mmarkRead[39m,
  [38;5;153mmarkAllRead[39m. Every method returns a Promise even though [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is synchronous — that's the whole point, so the
  HTTP adapter is a drop-in.

  [1mAdapters[0m ([38;5;153msrc/adapters/[39m) — [38;5;153mlocal-storage-repository.js[39m today; [38;5;153mhttp-repository.js[39m later, implementing the same
  contract. Also [38;5;153mchannels/in-app.js[39m, the sole registered delivery channel. A [38;5;153mdeliver(notification)[39m dispatcher iterates
  registered channels, so adding email means writing one file and registering it.

  [1mUI[0m ([38;5;153msrc/ui/[39m) — task list, task detail with a Follow/Mute control, and the notification bell + panel. Talks only to an
  app-service layer, never to adapters directly.

  The seam that matters: [38;5;153mresolveSubscribers[39m and the fan-out rules never learn where data lives. When the server arrives,
  [38;5;153madapters/[39m is the only directory that changes.

  [1mTooling[0m — the repo has none, and this is the cheapest moment it will ever be to add it. My recommendation: ESLint +
  Prettier, and Vitest with the domain core as the first test target (it's pure functions, so it's the highest-value,
  lowest-friction place to start). I'd skip E2E and fuzz/mutation testing for now — there's no UI worth driving yet, and
  they'd be scaffolding without a subject.
